---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import data from "../data/data.json";
import { pct } from "../lib/format";
import { modelMeta } from "../lib/vendors";
import { withBase } from "../lib/paths";

const { meta, models } = data;
const slms = models.filter((m) => m.type === "SLM");
const llms = models.filter((m) => m.type === "LLM");
---
<Base title={`About · ${meta.title}`} active="about" description="Why SecureCode Arena exists, what it measures, and who conducted the study.">
  <article class="article prose">
    <h1>A defensive benchmark for generated code</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      SecureCode Arena measures how often AI coding models introduce security vulnerabilities
      when they are asked only for useful functionality. It does not measure how well a model can
      find or exploit bugs.
    </p>
    <div class="cover-img-wrap">
      <img src={withBase("/cover.png")} alt="SecureCode Arena — AI models competing to write secure code" class="cover-img" />
    </div>

    <h2>What the study measures</h2>
    <p>
      Each of {meta.models_total} models answered the same {meta.prompts_total} prompts three times
      ({meta.files_total} files). After a parse and compile gate ({meta.files_valid} valid,
      {meta.files_invalid} invalid), remaining files were scanned with {meta.tools.join(", ")}.
      Findings were deduplicated on (CWE, line) and mapped to severity.
    </p>
    <p>
      The headline number is <strong>secure rate</strong>: the share of prompts with no targeted
      HIGH or MEDIUM finding in any repetition. Four cloud models tie at 14/16 secure
      ({pct(0.875)}). The three local SLMs are each secure on 11/16.
    </p>
    <p>
      Functional correctness is not scored. A program that compiles and still concatenates SQL
      is a vulnerability. A program that fails to parse is invalid, not secure.
    </p>

    <h2>The gap this fills</h2>
    <p>
      Hasan et al. (2026) evaluated 20 SLMs (0.4B to 10B) on HumanEval, MBPP, Mercury, HumanEvalPack
      and CodeXGLUE. They reported pass@k, VRAM, latency, and multilingual performance.
      Section 5.1 of that paper states that those metrics do not account for the security of
      generated code. A function can pass every unit test and still contain command injection.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concurrent work on secure code generation at agent scale, including LSCBench
      (Wu et al., 2026), likewise finds that functional success far outruns security. This study
      is complementary: it focuses on small local models versus cloud assistants, under a
      static-analysis protocol, with a Qwen size ladder.
    </p>
    <p>
      The SLM versus LLM comparison is <em>descriptive</em>. Three local SLMs and six cloud LLMs
      is too small for significance tests. We report means: SLM mean targeted HIGH/MEDIUM rate
      {meta.slm_mean_vuln_rate}, LLM mean {meta.llm_mean_vuln_rate}.
    </p>

    <h2>Models tested</h2>
    <p>
      Three local SLMs via Ollama on CPU, and six cloud LLMs via browser chat. Temperature 0.2
      for SLMs. Browser defaults (unknown) for LLMs. Fresh chat per generation.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Model</th><th>Type</th><th>Size</th><th>Vendor</th><th>Weights</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {[...slms, ...llms].map((m) => {
          const mm = modelMeta(m.id);
          return (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={withBase(`/models/${m.slug}/`)}>{m.name}</a></td>
              <td>{m.type}</td>
              <td>{m.size ?? "n/a"}</td>
              <td>{mm.vendor}</td>
              <td>{mm.weights === "open" ? "Open" : "Proprietary"}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The Qwen ladder is intentional: Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B, then 7B, then Qwen3-Coder Next (LLM).
      Llama 3.1 8B is a general-purpose control, not a code specialist.
    </p>

    <h2>Languages and projects</h2>
    <p>
      Python, Go and JavaScript. Hasan et al. found SLMs stronger on Python and weaker on Go.
      Six coding projects (authentication API, file service, database search, health monitor, session
      handler, plus a Python-only Fibonacci control) yield 16 prompts. Details live on
      <a href={withBase("/projects/")}>Projects</a> and <a href={withBase("/methodology/")}>Methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Authors</h2>
    <p>
      This benchmark is an academic research project at the National Institute of Posts and
      Telecommunications (INPT), Rabat.
    </p>
    <div class="team-grid">
      <div class="team-card" style="grid-column: 1 / -1; max-width: 320px; margin: 0 auto;">
        <strong>Meryeme Ayache</strong>
        <div class="role">Associate Professor · Supervisor</div>
      </div>
      <div class="team-card">
        <strong>Mohamed Anaddam</strong>
        <div class="role">Cybersecurity Engineer</div>
      </div>
      <div class="team-card">
        <strong>Maryam Iblihi</strong>
        <div class="role">Cybersecurity Engineer</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Citing this work</h2>
    <p>
      Anaddam, M., Iblihi, M., and Ayache, M. (2026). SecureCode Arena: a defensive benchmark of
      AI-generated code security. INPT.
    </p>
    <pre class="cite">@misc&#123;securecodearena2026,
  author       = &#123;Anaddam, Mohamed and Iblihi, Maryam and Ayache, Meryeme&#125;,
  title        = &#123;&#123;SecureCode Arena&#125;: A Defensive Benchmark of &#123;AI&#125;-Generated Code Security&#125;,
  year         = &#123;2026&#125;,
  institution  = &#123;INPT&#125;,
  note         = &#123;v1.0&#125;
&#125;</pre>

    <h2>Related work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Hasan, M. M., et al. (2026). Evaluation of small language models on code generation.
        Cited for the SLM correctness and efficiency gap that this study addresses.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wu, T., et al. (2026).
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14766">Last Secure Code Benchmark: Is the Code You Vibe Secure?</a>
        arXiv:2610.14766. Agent-scale secure code generation across function, file and repository tasks.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
